.01010894775390625, 0.0150146484375, 0.03082275390625, 0.035797119140625, -0.01519012451171875, -0.0165863037109375, 0.00478363037109375, -0.019622802734375, 0.0031261444091796875, -0.01284027099609375, 0.00868988037109375, -0.00012695789337158203, 0.014129638671875, -0.0202178955078125, 0.01203155517578125, 0.0394287109375, 0.0168609619140625, 0.00664520263671875, -0.021148681640625, 0.01110076904296875, -0.013275146484375, 0.0186767578125, -0.017913818359375, 0.0057373046875, -0.01030731201171875, -0.0308837890625, 0.00661468505859375, 0.0015535354614257812, 0.0005731582641601562, -0.00850677490234375, 0.039642333984375, 0.01263427734375, 0.009765625, -0.0223846435546875, 0.0087738037109375, 0.01476287841796875, -0.00464630126953125, -0.004581451416015625, -0.00736236572265625, -0.028076171875, 0.0051116943359375, -0.01490020751953125, -0.002483367919921875, -0.0032024383544921875, -0.018341064453125, 0.0200347900390625, -0.050750732421875, 0.002201080322265625, -0.022125244140625, -0.033782958984375, 0.01509857177734375, -0.0333251953125, -0.00388336181640625, -0.0139312744140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. This work addresses the following research questions: (1) How robust are memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order? (2) To what extent does task and environment specification affect agent performance and reliability? (3) What evaluation protocols and system designs can mitigate observed fragility?\n\n**Background**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Replay (EMR), where agents store and retrieve past experiences to inform future actions, and (b) Memory-Augmented Policy Optimization (MAPO), where agents maintain a memory of successful trajectories to guide learning. These methods have been widely adopted in recent self-improving agent literature.\n\n**Methods**\n\nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs (10 independent seeds per configuration) to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Experiments are conducted in two complex simulated environments: a multi-step navigation task and a compositional instruction-following benchmark, each comprising 20 distinct tasks. For each agent-method pair, we report mean and standard deviation of success rates across runs and task orderings.\n\nTo better understand the sources of fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory banks after training. This analysis involves reviewing stored trajectories and textual summaries, using a rubric that assesses (i) completeness of task representation, (ii) relevance of retrieved memories to current tasks, and (iii) consistency of memory updates. Two annotators independently coded 50 randomly sampled memory entries per agent, resolving disagreements through discussion.\n\n**Results**\n\nOur experiments yield two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: for example, the standard deviation of success rates across runs reaches up to 18% for EMR and 15% for MAPO in the navigation environment. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, with some runs failing to improve at all. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. When tasks are shuffled, average performance drops by 12\u201320% compared to the default curriculum order, and in some cases, agents fail to solve later tasks entirely.\n\nManual examination of memory banks reveals that agents often store incomplete or ambiguous task representations, and retrieval frequently surfaces irrelevant or outdated experiences. We hypothesize that task and environment underspecification contribute to this fragility. To validate this, we incorporate additional information\u2014such as detailed rubrics specifying success criteria and richer environment feedback\u2014into the memory construction process. This intervention partially closes the performance gap: for example, the average success rate under shuffled task order improves by 7% for EMR and 6% for MAPO, but significant gaps (5\u201310% lower than default order) still remain.\n\n**Discussion**\n\nThese results suggest that other uncharacterized factors, such as memory interference, insufficient exploration, or limitations in the agents' retrieval mechanisms, may also contribute to fragility. Future work could investigate the impact of memory management strategies, adaptive curriculum learning, or hybrid human-in-the-loop oversight to further improve robustness.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nLooking ahead, we advocate for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, including: (1) reporting results across multiple independent runs and random task orderings, (2) publishing detailed experimental setups and seeds for reproducibility, and (3) stress-testing agents under challenging and adversarial conditions. To address underspecification, we recommend developing systems and interfaces that facilitate effective human oversight, such as interactive memory inspection tools, annotation workflows for specifying task requirements, and mechanisms for human intervention when agents encounter ambiguous or novel situations. These steps can help prevent agents from failing in unforeseeable ways and advance the reliability of memory-based self-improving agents."}}